{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Weight for weight shall it be.</b> He did not specify the weight of each one as he specified for the anointing oil, but explained that they should all be in equal measure, this one like that one. And the reason he mentioned only four of the eleven is that Scripture is particular only about these four, whose columns of smoke rise abundantly, whereas the other spices have no such requirement." "<b>And the Ramban</b> explained that what it says, ״Take for yourself spices,״ means the spices already mentioned, and it refers back to the spices in the anointing oil — for סמים and בשמים are the same thing, and all the spices in the making of the oil entered into the incense. Now there were in the making of the incense nine of those specified in Scripture: five from the anointing oil and four in the incense. And Scripture added a second time ״סמים״ to add to the nine mentioned other spices, namely שבולת נרד (spikenard) and כרכם (crocus/saffron), and Scripture called the making of the incense טהור וקדש (pure and holy)." "<b>Nataf and shechelet and chelbanah.</b> Nataf is צרי (styrax/benzoin), and Rashi, of blessed memory, explained צרי as טריא״ק (theriac). And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that this is not correct, for nataf is a single spice on its own, like shechelet and chelbanah and levonah; whereas theriac is a compound in which many ingredients are combined with one another, and into which they put leaven and honey and powder of burnt scorpions and the flesh of the viper — and far be it that they should put into the incense, which Scripture called ״טהור קדש״ — pure and holy — the flesh of creeping things and reptiles, nor leaven and honey, concerning which Scripture says (Leviticus 2:11): ״כי כל שאור וכל דבש״ and so on. Rather, the nataf, which is צרי, is called by one distinctive name, and it is the resin of the קטף trees; the tree is called קטף because when they pluck (קוטפין) the branch, the sap drips, which is like an oil dripping from it from the place of plucking. And this is what they said, our Rabbis of blessed memory: Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says, the צרי is nothing but the resin that comes out of the קטף trees. And it is a very distinguished spice, for our Rabbis of blessed memory forbade it in the chapter במה מדליקין to light from it, and they said: one may not light with צרי — and the reason, on account of its excellence and preciousness, was a decree lest one come to be misappropriating it. And חלבנה has a foul odor, yet Scripture included it among the distinguished spices, to hint that we should not take lightly the wicked among the transgressors of Israel, that they should not be included in our fasts and our prayers — as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: any באגודה (bound group) that does not include the transgressors of Israel is not an אגודה. And the explanation of this is that the Name of Heaven is exalted and sanctified when the wicked return in repentance and are counted among the righteous, for if not, the righteous would be held accountable on their account because of ערבות (suretyship/ mutual responsibility), since all Israel are guarantors for one another. And for this reason we were commanded in the mitzvah of lulav to take four species in one אגודה (binding) and to include the ערבה (willow), which has no taste and no fragrance — a hint to the wicked of Israel who have no Torah and no mitzvot — together with the אתרוג (etrog), which has taste and fragrance, and together with the לולב (lulav), which has food, and the הדס (myrtle), which has fragrance; and we appease the Holy One, blessed be He, with all of them as one."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:56.448331170Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}